WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.607 --> 00:00:11.051
In 1868, John Muir traveled to the Yosemite Valley in the Sierra Nevada Mountains of California.

2
00:00:11.712 --> 00:00:16.415
When he saw it, he wrote, "No temple made with human hands can compare with Yosemite."

3
00:00:16.996 --> 00:00:22.319
Muir was a pioneer in the science of ecology—the study of living things and their environments.

4
00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:28.864
He was also one of the first people to promote "ecotourism"—a combination of "ecology" and "tourism."

5
00:00:29.712 --> 00:00:38.415
In 1892, he founded an environmental organization called the Sierra Club, with the following motto: "Explore, enjoy, and

6
00:00:38.435 --> 00:00:39.335
protect the planet."

7
00:00:39.916 --> 00:00:45.597
Muir knew how important natural wonders were to the human spirit, and he encouraged the government to protect

8
00:00:45.718 --> 00:00:47.898
untouched areas for future generations.

9
00:00:48.759 --> 00:00:52.600
But careless people can damage the wilderness they wish to experience.

10
00:00:53.820 --> 00:01:00.324
In Muir's beloved Yosemite, for instance, the enormous Wawona Tree was nearly two thousand years old when a

11
00:01:00.365 --> 00:01:02.866
tunnel big enough for a car was cut through its trunk.

12
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:08.530
The tree died not even a century later; the weakened trunk couldn't support the weight of the snow

13
00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.412
on its branches after a storm in 1969.

14
00:01:12.392 --> 00:01:17.876
Since the Wawona Tree fell, however, many people have realized the impact their travels can have on the

15
00:01:17.916 --> 00:01:24.388
environment. Although tourism has existed ever since people have had free time and money to spend, during the

16
00:01:24.508 --> 00:01:27.989
1980s, the idea of ecotourism finally took hold.

17
00:01:28.649 --> 00:01:35.331
Its goals are to provide experiences in nature that educate the public, protect the environment, and ensure the

18
00:01:35.351 --> 00:01:37.092
prosperity of indigenous people.

19
00:01:37.872 --> 00:01:43.353
Ecologists felt that if people had a better understanding of the planet, they would work harder to protect

20
00:01:43.453 --> 00:01:51.035
it. They encouraged activities that allow people to learn about a variety of ecosystems and experience wildlife up

21
00:01:51.095 --> 00:01:53.336
close—without damaging the environment.

22
00:01:54.428 --> 00:02:01.213
Before ecotourism, many tourists would flock to interesting or relaxing places without a thought about the consequences of

23
00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:01.914
their actions.

24
00:02:02.734 --> 00:02:08.799
For example, Cancún Island, a narrow strip of land off the eastern coast of Mexico, served as a

25
00:02:08.839 --> 00:02:10.941
nesting site for sea turtles and seabirds.

26
00:02:11.741 --> 00:02:15.524
But in the 1970s, a resort was built to attract vacationers.

27
00:02:16.425 --> 00:02:20.488
Roads and golf courses followed, and soon, luxury hotels lined the beach.

28
00:02:21.152 --> 00:02:25.174
Crowds packed the resort area, seriously affecting the island's environment.

29
00:02:25.895 --> 00:02:32.398
Garbage piled up, sewage and chemicals contaminated the local lagoon, and sunscreen from the bodies of thousands of

30
00:02:32.438 --> 00:02:35.020
swimmers began to destroy the nearby coral reef.

31
00:02:36.121 --> 00:02:42.164
Ecotourism, on the other hand, offers some exciting alternatives to lying on a beach with thousands of other

32
00:02:42.204 --> 00:02:50.049
vacationers. Ecotourists can experience the wilderness and help maintain it by clearing old hiking trails while nature guides

33
00:02:50.109 --> 00:02:51.210
lead them through the forest.

34
00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:59.116
Working and camping on the trails, visitors learn firsthand what the slogan "Leave nothing but footprints, take nothing

35
00:02:59.156 --> 00:03:00.858
but photographs" really means.

36
00:03:01.839 --> 00:03:07.944
Rafting is another great way to interact with nature while leaving no trace on the river ecosystem, and

37
00:03:07.984 --> 00:03:11.467
because no motor is used, rafting conserves energy too.

38
00:03:12.628 --> 00:03:19.064
Ecotourists can also learn about the environment and help to preserve fragile ecosystems by visiting the world's rain

39
00:03:19.104 --> 00:03:26.287
forests. The Central American country of Costa Rica, for example, features trams and walkways suspended in the jungle's

40
00:03:26.327 --> 00:03:33.349
canopy. Visitors can ride or walk among the treetops and see birds, large mammals, and plants that collect

41
00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:35.690
water for the creatures living high in the trees.

42
00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:43.512
The money generated by vacationers supports rain-forest conservation and allows activists to fight deforestation.

43
00:03:44.348 --> 00:03:50.492
Travelers looking for adventure can experience Africa's biodiversity and contribute to protecting its wildlife.

44
00:03:51.093 --> 00:03:56.636
While tracking gorillas in the jungles of Rwanda, tourists can also catch sight of lions and elephants.

45
00:03:57.517 --> 00:04:02.160
Ecotourism companies hire indigenous people to act as guides for the tourists.

46
00:04:02.801 --> 00:04:08.564
When locals make an income by guiding and educating tourists attracted by the wildlife, the members of the

47
00:04:08.624 --> 00:04:13.608
community are more likely to protect the habitat and less likely to resort to poaching.

48
00:04:14.976 --> 00:04:20.780
To get a unique view of the Central Serengeti in Tanzania, visitors can fly above the African plain

49
00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:21.821
in a hot-air balloon.

50
00:04:22.581 --> 00:04:27.465
The largest wildlife migration in the world takes place here, so this is the only place on the

51
00:04:27.505 --> 00:04:33.369
planet visitors can witness herds of thousands of wildebeests and zebras passing in search of new pastures.

52
00:04:34.169 --> 00:04:39.192
Most people would say shooting a photograph on a safari is much better than shooting an animal.

53
00:04:39.893 --> 00:04:45.912
These sightseeing safaris have become a booming business for local tour operators, and the money the tourists bring

54
00:04:45.932 --> 00:04:48.593
in helps maintain the natural beauty of the area.

55
00:04:49.954 --> 00:04:56.316
The tourism industry has taken notice that as people become more environmentally conscious, they are willing to pay

56
00:04:56.376 --> 00:04:58.796
higher prices to vacation at "green" resorts.

57
00:04:59.697 --> 00:05:02.097
These places make the environment a priority.

58
00:05:02.738 --> 00:05:09.680
They try to use renewable resources, to conserve water, and to minimize their "carbon footprint"—the amount of greenhouse

59
00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:10.680
gases they produce.

60
00:05:11.632 --> 00:05:17.377
They may do this by using materials that can be replenished quickly, such as bamboo, instead of trees

61
00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:18.818
that take longer to grow back.

62
00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:24.723
Or they may use less water by giving guests the option of having their towels and sheets laundered

63
00:05:24.823 --> 00:05:26.905
every few days instead of daily.

64
00:05:27.746 --> 00:05:31.889
These practices also allow businesses to lower their energy consumption.

65
00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:37.254
By lessening their impact on the environment, resort owners can also increase their profits.

66
00:05:38.054 --> 00:05:43.162
Not only do their costs decrease, but green practices attract greater numbers of guests.

67
00:05:44.282 --> 00:05:49.986
Some unethical companies, however, take advantage of the public's willingness to spend a little extra to protect the

68
00:05:50.026 --> 00:05:57.210
planet. These companies mislead consumers by claiming to be green, even though their concern for the environment takes

69
00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.211
a back seat to their desire for profits.

70
00:06:00.011 --> 00:06:01.492
This is called "greenwashing."

71
00:06:02.113 --> 00:06:07.696
For example, in 2002, nature tourists paid to travel to a beach on the west coast of Africa

72
00:06:08.116 --> 00:06:09.937
to see sea turtles laying their eggs.

73
00:06:10.818 --> 00:06:17.662
Local activists watched with horror as the visitors drove along the beach on all-terrain vehicles, running over nests

74
00:06:17.722 --> 00:06:19.443
and rushing right up to the turtles.

75
00:06:20.343 --> 00:06:27.967
A spokeswoman for the International Fund for Animal Welfare criticized the tour's organizers, saying, "It was completely irresponsible.

76
00:06:28.548 --> 00:06:30.129
They didn't care about the animals."

77
00:06:31.249 --> 00:06:35.572
Many people do not understand the magnitude of humans' effect on sensitive habitats.

78
00:06:36.416 --> 00:06:41.598
But some people are aware of the dangers and are anxious to see the frontiers that are disappearing.

79
00:06:42.438 --> 00:06:49.420
On vacation, they travel to faraway places like Antarctica or Australia's Great Barrier Reef to see them before

80
00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:52.461
they become damaged beyond repair by global warming.

81
00:06:53.281 --> 00:06:56.202
Unfortunately, these trips often add to the problem.

82
00:06:56.862 --> 00:07:02.244
For instance, airplanes release large amounts of greenhouse gases and make global warming worse.

83
00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.483
Ecotourism has been a double-edged sword for the environment.

84
00:07:07.023 --> 00:07:13.488
On one hand, it has provided much-needed funds for the protection of endangered species and at-risk areas while

85
00:07:13.588 --> 00:07:14.569
educating the public.

86
00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:22.515
On the other hand, ecotourism has created a stampede into vulnerable areas of the world, hastening the destruction

87
00:07:22.555 --> 00:07:24.217
of many of these precious sites.

88
00:07:25.238 --> 00:07:30.982
Many hope that in the future, ecotourism will create even more chances for people to learn about nature,

89
00:07:31.422 --> 00:07:33.024
but in a more responsible way.

90
00:07:33.748 --> 00:07:39.219
Because the more people recognize how connected they are to the natural world, the more determined they will

91
00:07:39.259 --> 00:07:40.261
be to protect it.
